  "facts": [
    "The Hawthorne Bridge, opened December 19, 1910, is the oldest vertical‑lift bridge still operating in the United States, with a 244‑foot lift span raised an average of 120 times monthly for river traffic.",
    "In 1998, a $21 million renovation widened the sidewalks to 10 feet, turning the bridge into Oregon’s busiest bicycle crossing with over 8,000 cyclists daily and 800 TriMet buses carrying roughly 17,400 riders.",
    "Designed by Waddell & Harrington, the bridge’s 165‑foot towers hold 880,000‑pound counterweights, and its 150‑horsepower motors have powered the lift span since its 1910 debut."
